  <article class="research">
    <header>
      <p class="status">Work in Progress</p>
      <h1 class="title">Closed-Loop Auditory Stimulation via Frontal Electrode Data</h1>
    </header>

    <section>
      <p>Under what circumstances does a single frontal electrode provide a sufficiently reliable estimate of alpha or theta phase with respect to multichannel EEG data? Can a confidence gate use information gathered by a Fz electrode to determine when to trigger auditory stimulation, and what is the magnitude of coverage-accuracy tradeoff?</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>Closed loop auditory stimulation (henceforth: CLAS) delivers a sound according to the brain state measured immediately before the sound. In the <a href="#citation-jaramillo">Jaramillo et al. paper and dataset</a>, the signal from a frontal (Fz) electrode is filtered in either the alpha or theta band, depending on the stimulation block, and a sound is triggered according to the estimated phase of that oscillation. These phases are: peak (0°), falling (90°), trough (180°), or rising (270°). Simultaneously, Jaramillo et al. record data from a 128-channel high-density EEG. Thus, using this data, I compare the phase measured by the Fz electrode with the phase measured by the complete frontal electrode set excluding Fz.</p>
      <p>In this project, I describe quantitatively the circumstances in which the estimate from the Fz electrode is most likely to fall within 45° (i.e. the same categorization) as the broader electrode set, and test different gating rules to minimize the proportion of “incorrect” audio discharges (insofar as the complete electrode set is the source of truth) while still achieving high coverage. This could inform a simplified sleep wearable that only uses a frontal electrode to trigger audio stimulations, as opposed to a more costly complete EEG cap.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Hypotheses and Rationale</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>H1: Absolute circular phase error between Fz and the multichannel reference is higher for alpha than for theta cycles, as theta cycles are longer such that a fixed timing error occupies a smaller fraction of a cycle.</li>
        <li>H2: Absolute circular phase error between Fz and the multichannel reference is higher during phasic REM than tonic REM, as rapid eye movements may contaminate the data garnered by the frontal electrode during phasic REM.</li>
        <li>H3: Accuracy is a function of signal quality; phase estimates are least reliable when the target oscillation is weak or contaminated. Thus a controller that accepts only high-confidence Fz estimates achieves higher agreement with the multichannel reference than an ungated Fz estimate, although the former stimulates on a smaller proportion of opportunities.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Descriptive Analysis</h2>
      <p>The unit of analysis is an individual time-bound alpha-targeted or theta-targeted sound event. The primary outcome variable is the absolute circular phase error, measured in degrees, between the Fz estimate and the multichannel frontal reference, which excludes the frontal electrode, at the onset of the stimulation. The error mechanically ranges from 0 to 180 degrees. I am also considering a binary agreement indicator variable as a secondary outcome variable, which I construct using the absolute circular phase error; given that the experimental targets are 90° apart, this binary variable is coded as 1 when the Fz estimate is within 45° of the multichannel-measured phase, as 45° represent the midpoint between neighboring phase categories.</p>
      <p>The predictor variables include target rhythm, REM substage, Fz target-band amplitude, Fz spectral prominence relative to neighboring frequencies, recent Fz phase stability, target phase, and elapsed time in the recording. These are all variables that a frontal electrode could measure before stimulation.</p>
      <p>The control variables include participant identity; the intercept of the regression accounts for time-invariant entity-specific fixed effects. The data analysis excludes RSN_013 given technical problems, and excludes pre-stimulus windows with missing samples or empty Fz data. Participant data are not excluded based on observed Fz accuracy.</p>
